#!/usr/bin/env zsh
# Media stack daily checkup — runs once a day via LaunchAgent at 8am.
#
# Complements the existing 5-minute watchdog (container/NAS/Docker-level
# checks) and the 4am backup job with an APPLICATION-level pass that those
# can't see: *arr SQLite integrity (docker can report a container "healthy"
# while its database is silently corrupted at the btree level — this
# happened to Sonarr on 2026-07-31 and went undetected for weeks), VPN
# health, qBittorrent auth, indexer health, and stalled/rejected downloads
# (e.g. a quality profile rejecting the only releases that exist for old
# content).
#
# Delegates the actual diagnosis/fix to Claude Code (media-manager skill)
# since these failure modes need judgment, not a fixed script — a database
# recovery looks different every time depending on what's salvageable.
#
# Scope: restarts, auth resets, DB corruption repair (always backs up
# first), retriggered searches, and per-item quality profile fixes when a
# title has zero releases at its current floor. NEVER touches library
# content, orphan removal, or recycle bin cleanup — those still require a
# human. Alerts via the existing throttled email channel only when
# something was found or fixed; silent on a clean run.

set -uo pipefail

LOG="$HOME/logs/media-stack-daily-checkup.log"
PLEX_DIR="$HOME/dev/plex"
ALERT="$HOME/tools/media-stack-alert"
CLAUDE="$HOME/.local/bin/claude"

mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LOG")"
log() { echo "[$(date '+%F %T')] $1" >> "$LOG"; }

if [[ ! -x "$CLAUDE" ]]; then
  log "ERROR: claude binary not found at $CLAUDE"
  echo "claude binary missing at $CLAUDE — daily checkup could not run" | "$ALERT" "Daily checkup FAILED to start"
  exit 1
fi

log "=== Starting daily checkup ==="

PROMPT="$(cat <<'EOF'
Use the media-manager skill to run a full health check of my Plex media
stack, then fix anything broken. Run entirely non-interactively — this is
an unattended daily job, so make the reasonable call yourself rather than
asking questions; err on the side of NOT doing anything destructive if
genuinely unsure.

Beyond the skill's normal Health Check & Diagnostics / Fix Problems
workflow, specifically also do this, since it's a known gap in the
existing 5-minute container watchdog:

1. Run `sqlite3 -readonly <db> "PRAGMA integrity_check;"` directly against
   sonarr.db and radarr.db in their appdata dirs. A container can report
   "healthy" via Docker while the app's SQLite database is corrupted at
   the btree level (this happened to Sonarr's DB and went undetected for
   weeks because the health check never inspects the database itself).
2. If a database is corrupted: copy it (and its -wal/-shm) to a timestamped
   backup file in the same appdata dir BEFORE touching anything. Stop the
   container, run `sqlite3 <db> ".recover"` into a fresh database file,
   verify the fresh file passes `PRAGMA integrity_check`, swap it in,
   restart the container, then actually exercise the app's API (e.g. list
   series/movies, trigger a search) to confirm it works end to end — if a
   query fails referencing a specific column with malformed leftover data
   (this happened with IndexerStatus.LastRssSyncReleaseInfo containing
   truncated JSON), null out just that column's bad data and retry. Keep
   the corrupted backup; do not delete it.
3. For anything monitored with zero grabbed releases for longer than a few
   days: pull the release list and check rejections. If EVERY available
   release is being rejected by the quality profile (e.g. content old
   enough that no 1080p release exists anywhere, only 720p/DVD), and
   that's the ONLY reason nothing is downloading, it's fine to bump just
   that single item's quality profile down to one that accepts what's
   actually available, and re-trigger a search. Do not change the global
   default profile. If there are simply zero releases found anywhere
   (nothing to grab, obscure/unseeded content), that's not a bug — leave
   it monitored and move on, don't loop retrying it forever.

Explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for this unattended run — never do these, even if
you notice something that looks like an opportunity: removing orphaned
movies/shows, clearing the Synology recycle bin, deleting any files,
emptying Plex trash. Flag those in your summary for a human to review
instead of acting.

When you're done, send exactly ONE summary email via:
  ~/tools/media-stack-alert "<short subject: e.g. 'all healthy' or 'N issues found, N fixed'>"
piping in a short plain-text body listing what was checked, what (if
anything) was wrong, and what you did about it — including anything you
flagged as out-of-scope for a human to review. If literally everything
was healthy and nothing needed fixing, it is fine to skip sending an email
entirely (silence == all good, matching how the existing watchdog
behaves) — just log that in your final response instead.
EOF
)"

"$CLAUDE" -p "$PROMPT" \
  --permission-mode bypassPermissions \
  --model sonnet \
  --add-dir "$PLEX_DIR" \
  >> "$LOG" 2>&1
rc=$?

if (( rc != 0 )); then
  log "ERROR: claude exited with code $rc"
  tail -n 50 "$LOG" | "$ALERT" "Daily checkup FAILED (exit $rc)"
else
  log "=== Daily checkup finished (exit 0) ==="
fi

exit $rc
